🔥 Most Used Design Patterns in JavaScript & React

📌 Creational Patterns
(Focus on object creation mechanisms)
1. Singleton

Ensures a class has only one instance.
Used in state management (Redux, Zustand) and global configuration.

Example (JavaScript):
class Singleton {
  static #instance = null;

  constructor() {
    if (Singleton.#instance) return Singleton.#instance;
    Singleton.#instance = this;
  }

  static getInstance() {
    if (!Singleton.#instance) new Singleton();
    return Singleton.#instance;
  }
}

const instance1 = new Singleton();
const instance2 = Singleton.getInstance();
console.log(instance1 === instance2); // true
In React (using Context API):
import { createContext, useContext } from 'react';

const GlobalStateContext = createContext();

export const GlobalStateProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const state = { theme: 'dark', user: null };
  return (
    <GlobalStateContext.Provider value={state}>
      {children}
    </GlobalStateContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useGlobalState = () => useContext(GlobalStateContext);

2. Factory Method

Defines an interface for creating objects, but lets subclasses decide which class to instantiate.
Used in React component factories and dynamic UI rendering.

Example (JavaScript):
class Button {
  render() { return "<button>Click</button>"; }
}

class Link {
  render() { return "<a href='#'>Click</a>"; }
}

class ButtonFactory {
  createButton(type) {
    return type === 'link' ? new Link() : new Button();
  }
}

const factory = new ButtonFactory();
const button = factory.createButton('button');
console.log(button.render()); // "<button>Click</button>"
In React:
const ButtonFactory = ({ type }) => {
  const Button = type === 'primary' ? PrimaryButton : SecondaryButton;
  return <Button />;
};

3. Builder

Separates the construction of a complex object from its representation.
Used in React component composition and API request builders.

Example (JavaScript):
class Pizza {
  constructor() {
    this.toppings = [];
  }

  addTopping(topping) {
    this.toppings.push(topping);
    return this;
  }
}

const pizza = new Pizza()
  .addTopping('cheese')
  .addTopping('pepperoni')
  .addTopping('mushrooms');
In React:
const PizzaBuilder = ({ children }) => {
  const [toppings, setToppings] = useState([]);

  const addTopping = (topping) => {
    setToppings([...toppings, topping]);
  };

  return children({ toppings, addTopping });
};

// Usage:
<PizzaBuilder>
  {({ toppings, addTopping }) => (
    <div>
      {toppings.map(t => <span>{t}</span>)}
      <button onClick={() => addTopping('cheese')}>Add Cheese</button>
    </div>
  )}
</PizzaBuilder>

🔹 Structural Patterns
(Focus on object composition)
4. Adapter

Allows incompatible interfaces to work together.
Used in API wrappers and third-party library integration.

Example (JavaScript):
class OldAPI {
  fetchData() {
    return { data: [1, 2, 3] };
  }
}

class NewAPI {
  getData() {
    return { results: [4, 5, 6] };
  }
}

class APIAdapter {
  constructor(api) {
    this.api = api;
  }

  fetchData() {
    const data = this.api.getData();
    return { data: data.results };
  }
}

const oldApi = new OldAPI();
const newApi = new NewAPI();
const adapter = new APIAdapter(newApi);

console.log(adapter.fetchData()); // { data: [4, 5, 6] }
In React:
const ThirdPartyChart = ({ data }) => {
  // Assume this expects a different data format
  return <div>{JSON.stringify(data)}</div>;
};

const ChartAdapter = ({ data }) => {
  const adaptedData = { values: data };
  return <ThirdPartyChart data={adaptedData} />;
};

5. Decorator

Adds new behavior to objects dynamically.
Used in React HOCs (Higher-Order Components) and middleware.

Example (JavaScript):
class Coffee {
  cost() { return 5; }
}

class MilkDecorator {
  constructor(coffee) {
    this.coffee = coffee;
  }

  cost() {
    return this.coffee.cost() + 2;
  }
}

const coffee = new Coffee();
const coffeeWithMilk = new MilkDecorator(coffee);
console.log(coffeeWithMilk.cost()); // 7
In React (HOC):
const withLogger = (Component) => {
  return (props) => {
    console.log('Props:', props);
    return <Component {...props} />;
  };
};

const MyComponent = () => <div>Hello</div>;
const EnhancedComponent = withLogger(MyComponent);

6. Facade

Provides a simplified interface to a complex subsystem.
Used in API services and utility libraries.

Example (JavaScript):
class CPU {
  execute() { console.log("Executing..."); }
}

class Memory {
  load() { console.log("Loading..."); }
}

class ComputerFacade {
  constructor() {
    this.cpu = new CPU();
    this.memory = new Memory();
  }

  start() {
    this.memory.load();
    this.cpu.execute();
  }
}

const computer = new ComputerFacade();
computer.start(); // "Loading...", "Executing..."
In React:
const ApiFacade = {
  fetchUser: async (id) => {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/users/${id}`);
    return response.json();
  },
  fetchPosts: async (userId) => {
    const response = await fetch(`/api/posts?userId=${userId}`);
    return response.json();
  },
};

// Usage:
const user = await ApiFacade.fetchUser(1);
const posts = await ApiFacade.fetchPosts(1);

🔹 Behavioral Patterns
(Focus on communication between objects)
7. Observer

Defines a one-to-many dependency between objects.
Used in React state management (useState, useReducer) and event emitters.

Example (JavaScript):
class Subject {
  constructor() {
    this.observers = [];
  }

  subscribe(observer) {
    this.observers.push(observer);
  }

  notify(data) {
    this.observers.forEach(observer => observer.update(data));
  }
}

class Observer {
  update(data) {
    console.log(`Received: ${data}`);
  }
}

const subject = new Subject();
const observer1 = new Observer();
const observer2 = new Observer();

subject.subscribe(observer1);
subject.subscribe(observer2);
subject.notify("Hello"); // "Received: Hello" (twice)
In React:
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const useObserver = (subject) => {
  const [data, setData] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const observer = (newData) => setData(newData);
    subject.subscribe(observer);
    return () => subject.unsubscribe(observer);
  }, [subject]);

  return data;
};

// Usage:
const subject = new Subject();
const MyComponent = () => {
  const data = useObserver(subject);
  return <div>{data}</div>;
};

8. Strategy

Defines a family of algorithms, encapsulates each one, and makes them interchangeable.
Used in React form validation and payment gateways.

Example (JavaScript):
class PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount) { throw new Error("Not implemented"); }
}

class CreditCardStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount) { return `Paid $${amount} via Credit Card`; }
}

class PayPalStrategy extends PaymentStrategy {
  pay(amount) { return `Paid $${amount} via PayPal`; }
}

class ShoppingCart {
  constructor(strategy) {
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  checkout(amount) {
    return this.strategy.pay(amount);
  }
}

const cart1 = new ShoppingCart(new CreditCardStrategy());
console.log(cart1.checkout(100)); // "Paid $100 via Credit Card"

const cart2 = new ShoppingCart(new PayPalStrategy());
console.log(cart2.checkout(50)); // "Paid $50 via PayPal"
In React:
const PaymentForm = ({ strategy }) => {
  const [amount, setAmount] = useState(0);

  const handleSubmit = () => {
    alert(strategy.pay(amount));
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input type="number" value={amount} onChange={(e) => setAmount(e.target.value)} />
      <button type="submit">Pay</button>
    </form>
  );
};

// Usage:
<PaymentForm strategy={new CreditCardStrategy()} />

9. Command

Encapsulates a request as an object, allowing for parameterization of clients.
Used in React undo/redo functionality and event handling.

Example (JavaScript):
class Command {
  execute() { throw new Error("Not implemented"); }
}

class LightOnCommand extends Command {
  execute() { console.log("Light is ON"); }
}

class LightOffCommand extends Command {
  execute() { console.log("Light is OFF"); }
}

class RemoteControl {
  submit(command) {
    command.execute();
  }
}

const remote = new RemoteControl();
remote.submit(new LightOnCommand()); // "Light is ON"
remote.submit(new LightOffCommand()); // "Light is OFF"
In React:
import { useReducer } from 'react';

const reducer = (state, action) => {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'TOGGLE_LIGHT':
      return { lightOn: !state.lightOn };
    default:
      return state;
  }
};

const LightSwitch = () => {
  const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, { lightOn: false });

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => dispatch({ type: 'TOGGLE_LIGHT' })}>
        Toggle Light
      </button>
      <p>{state.lightOn ? 'ON' : 'OFF'}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

10. Proxy

Provides a placeholder for another object to control access to it.
Used in lazy loading, caching, and API mocking.

Example (JavaScript):
class RealImage {
  display() { console.log("Displaying image"); }
}

class ProxyImage {
  constructor() {
    this.realImage = null;
  }

  display() {
    if (!this.realImage) {
      this.realImage = new RealImage();
    }
    this.realImage.display();
  }
}

const image = new ProxyImage();
image.display(); // "Displaying image" (only loaded once)
In React:
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

const ImageComponent = ({ src }) => {
  const [image, setImage] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const loadImage = async () => {
      const img = new Image();
      img.src = src;
      await img.decode();
      setImage(img);
    };
    loadImage();
  }, [src]);

  return image ? <img src={src} alt="Loaded" /> : <div>Loading...</div>;
};

